-**Parameters:**
-*   $M$: Number of sub-pixels for MSAA, set to 4 [S1].
-
-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Color Overflow:** If all triangles overlapping a pixel are treated as a single entity when Gaussians are present between them, the transmittance for deeper triangles does not account for decay from Gaussians, resulting in white artifacts [S1]. The solution is to treat only depth-adjacent triangles as a single entity [S1].
-*   **Precision:** The sub-pixel transmittance calculation $t_{ji}$ involves products of many terms, which may suffer from precision issues if many fragments overlap a single sub-pixel [S1].
+**Pitfalls**
+*   **Color Overflow:** If all triangles overlapping a pixel are treated as a single entity without considering Gaussians between them, the transmittance for deeper triangles does not account for decay from intervening Gaussians. This results in excessive brightness (white artifacts) [S1]. The solution is to treat only depth-adjacent triangles as a single entity for the $\mathbf{C}_{tris}$ calculation [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of fragments $N$ per pixel, $O(N)$, due to the single pass depth sort and accumulation [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the fragment list and sub-pixel transmittance states [S1].
-*   **Performance:** The method ensures correct spatial occlusion and color blending without dropping rendering rates compared to separate passes [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Unified handling of Gaussians and triangles; anti-aliased via MSAA; prevents color overflow artifacts [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful handling of depth-adjacency for triangles; sub-pixel transmittance tracking adds complexity [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of fragments per pixel. The sub-pixel coverage calculation adds a constant factor of $M$ (typically 4) to triangle processing.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing depth-sorted fragments per pixel. Sub-pixel coverage requires $M$ bits or flags per fragment per pixel.
+*   **Strengths:** Unified handling of meshes and Gaussians; correct occlusion; anti-aliased triangles via MSAA integration.
+*   **Limitations:** Complexity in managing depth-adjacent triangle groups to prevent artifacts; potential performance overhead from sub-pixel sampling if not optimized.
 
 ## Background
-This method was proposed in "UniMGS: Unifying Mesh and 3D Gaussian Splatting with Single-Pass Rasterization and Proxy-Based Deformation" [S1]. It addresses the limitations of proxy-based 3DGS deformation and the need for unified rendering of mesh and 3DGS in dynamic scenes [S1].
+This method was proposed in "UniMGS: Unifying Mesh and 3D Gaussian Splatting with Single-Pass Rasterization and Proxy-Based Deformation" to address the need for unified rendering of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) and mesh-based representations in dynamic scenes [S1]. It departs from proxy-based deformation strategies that are sensitive to mesh topology by using a Gaussian-centric strategy and a single-pass rasterization pipeline [S1].
 
